---
title: Hosting images and fonts
name: Hosting images and fonts
description: Upload the images and fonts built email HTML points at on disk to Postboi, kept for good, and rewrite the HTML to the hosted copies, whichever provider sends it.
category: Guides
---

An email loads its images and fonts from wherever its HTML says, for as long as it sits in
someone's inbox, so they need a public home that never moves. Postboi hosts them. One
command uploads the files your built HTML points at on disk and rewrites the HTML to the
hosted copies, whichever provider sends the email:

```bash
bunx postboi assets dist/*.html
```

```
assets   14 local (2 uploaded, 12 already there) in 6 files
```

## What it does

For each file, `assets` finds every local reference (`src`, `srcset`, `background`,
`url(...)` in styles and VML inside `<!--[if mso]>` comments), uploads the files and
rewrites the file in place to point at the copies. It's the same lookup as
[`testing run`](/email-testing#local-images-and-fonts): each path is looked up next to the
HTML, then in each folder above it up to the project root, then in the current directory,
with `testing.assets` in `postboi.config.ts` to pin the folder for `/…` paths. URLs,
Liquid and merge tags are left alone, and a file it can't find is warned about and left as
it is. PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP, AVIF, SVG and ICO images and WOFF, WOFF2, TTF and OTF fonts
are uploaded, up to 10 MB each.

The copies are named by a hash of the file. An unchanged image is never sent twice, and an
edited one gets a new URL, so mail that already went out keeps showing the old one. One
run uploads once for all its files, so a logo in every email goes up once. The copies
don't expire (test uploads do, after 30 days), and they share a store with
[web versions](/views), so an image both use is kept once.

`-` reads the HTML from stdin and writes the rewritten HTML to stdout, with the summary and
any warnings on stderr:

```bash
bunx postboi assets - < email.html > email.hosted.html
```

Hosting needs a verified sending domain or a paid plan, the same as publishing a web
version; without one the command stops with `views_not_allowed` and changes no file.

## In a build script

Run it on the built output rather than the source, so your templates keep their local
paths and still preview locally:

```json
{
	"scripts": {
		"build": "maizzle build && postboi assets dist/*.html"
	}
}
```

## Sending a file

`postboi send --file email.html` does the same before it sends: the local images and fonts
go up to the same store and the message goes out pointing at them. The file on disk keeps
its own paths. `--no-assets` sends the HTML as it is. `testing run` uploads to the test
store instead, which expires after 30 days.

## On your own domain

Once your team's view host is on (`mail-view.<your domain>`, see
[web versions](/views#on-your-own-domain)), the URLs are on it, like
`https://mail-view.example.com/assets/3f2a….png`, so images and fonts load from your own
domain. Without one they are on Postboi's: `https://postboi.app/views/assets/<team>/3f2a….png`.
Mail sent earlier keeps the URLs it was sent with, and both keep working.

Turning the view host off breaks the images and fonts in every email already sent with
URLs on it, so once mail has gone out that way, leave it on.
